            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Not *exactly* what you are looking for, but you can do anything inside [`config.hooks.pre`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/12d275f4c3aeddfa12a758a69aff65090613ca34/doc/mini-comment.txt#L110). Here is [an example of always yanking commented text](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/issues/701#issuecomment-1948501258).\r\n\r\nI am not sure how to properly achieve similar with built-in commenting. In Visual mode it can be done with `:xmap gy ygvgc` (`y` - yank visual selection, `gv` - restore visual selection, `gc` - comment visual selection). Note that it needs `remap = true` to achieve this with Lua's `vim.keymap.set()`.",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">Not <em>exactly</em> what you are looking for, but you can do anything inside <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/12d275f4c3aeddfa12a758a69aff65090613ca34/doc/mini-comment.txt#L110\"><code class=\"notranslate\">config.hooks.pre</code></a>. Here is <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/issues/701#issuecomment-1948501258\" data-hovercard-type=\"issue\" data-hovercard-url=\"/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/701/hovercard\">an example of always yanking commented text</a>.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I am not sure how to properly achieve similar with built-in commenting. In Visual mode it can be done with <code class=\"notranslate\">:xmap gy ygvgc</code> (<code class=\"notranslate\">y</code> - yank visual selection, <code class=\"notranslate\">gv</code> - restore visual selection, <code class=\"notranslate\">gc</code> - comment visual selection). Note that it needs <code class=\"notranslate\">remap = true</code> to achieve this with Lua's <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.keymap.set()</code>.</p>",
            "bodyText": "Not exactly what you are looking for, but you can do anything inside config.hooks.pre. Here is an example of always yanking commented text.\nI am not sure how to properly achieve similar with built-in commenting. In Visual mode it can be done with :xmap gy ygvgc (y - yank visual selection, gv - restore visual selection, gc - comment visual selection). Note that it needs remap = true to achieve this with Lua's vim.keymap.set().",
